merge_law.py: the accumulation design's laws, on an executable model

CHECK L1 merge order and grouping do not change coverage (commutative, associative)                    ok (3000 scenarios, 0 violations)
CHECK L2 a second device's copy of every record (distinct records, the same slices) does not change coverage ok (0 violations)
CHECK L3 coverage from interval union equals an independent bitmap of the same records                 ok (0 violations)
CHECK L4 adding a record never reduces coverage (monotone)                                             ok (0 violations)
CHECK L5 a record whose case count is wrong, or that found a mismatch, is not coverage                 ok (0 violations)
CHECK L6 records of one subject class (RTL build) never count for another                              ok (0 violations)
CHECK L7 coverage never exceeds the space although the cases RUN may                                   ok (0 violations)
CHECK L7' ten devices on one slice: cases run = 10 N, coverage = N                                     ok 
CHECK the correct fold credits build A with nothing when only build B ran                              ok 

CHECK PLANT first     breaks L1: the same test reports violations                                      ok (3000 of 3000 scenarios; also broken: L3 3000)
CHECK PLANT naive     breaks L2: the same test reports violations                                      ok (3000 of 3000 scenarios; also broken: L3 3000, L7 3000)
CHECK PLANT badmerge  breaks L3: the same test reports violations                                      ok (3000 of 3000 scenarios; also broken: L4 138)
CHECK PLANT overwrite breaks L4: the same test reports violations                                      ok (772 of 3000 scenarios; also broken: L1 684, L3 691)
CHECK PLANT nocount   breaks L5: the same test reports violations                                      ok (3000 of 3000 scenarios; also broken: L3 2544)
CHECK PLANT novm      breaks L5: the same test reports violations                                      ok (3000 of 3000 scenarios; also broken: L3 2558)
CHECK PLANT blind     breaks L6: the same test reports violations                                      ok (2897 of 3000 scenarios; also broken: L3 3000)
CHECK PLANT naive     breaks L7: the same test reports violations                                      ok (3000 of 3000 scenarios; also broken: L2 3000, L3 3000)
CHECK PLANT naive on the ten devices of L7': coverage above the space                                  ok 
CHECK PLANT blind on build B's half-space slice credits half the space to a build that never ran a case ok 

CHECK L8 two devices with identical outputs have identical roots                                       ok 
CHECK L8 one differing batch changes the root and is found by descent (300 trials, 4,096 batches)      ok (0 wrong)
CHECK L8 the descent used at most 2 log2(n) + 2 = 26 comparisons, against 4,096 for a scan             ok (worst 13)
CHECK with two differing batches the descent finds the FIRST (batch 7), and the rest by repeating on the remainder ok 
CHECK PLANT a descent that always goes left breaks L8: the same trials find the wrong batch            ok (300 of 300 wrong)
CHECK PLANT a root comparison alone says 'differs' but not where (the descent is what localizes)       ok 

CHECK L9 an honest slice replays from its record and its campaign record alone                         ok 
CHECK L9 a tampered slice is found by replay and its batch named                                       ok (differs at batch 5)
CHECK L9 without the campaign record replay refuses by name (the cid is a hash, its preimage is not in the slice record) ok 
CHECK L9 a campaign record that does not hash to the cid is refused by name                            ok 
CHECK PLANT a replay that does not check the campaign record against the cid calls an honest slice tampered, not a bad campaign record ok (differs at batch 0)
CHECK a campaign record is not coverage: a store holding only it covers nothing                        ok 

R. a record as section 5.3 writes it, canonical JSON: 1223 bytes; a weekend of 3287 slices 4.0 MB; a decade of 520 weekends 2.09 GB
   a campaign record (once for every slice that names it): 288 bytes
CHECK a decade of weekly weekends from one device is under 5 GB of records (the study's 'not a size problem') ok (1223 bytes a record, 2.09 GB)

merge_law: every CHECK held
